  "analysis": "From the problem and examples, the minimal finish time after processing all requests up to i can be computed by a simple formula. For every position k in [1..N], let S_k be the total number of products that must be started on or before time k and still finish no later than k (this translates to the sum of p_j over all requests j with a_j <= k <= b_j). The makespan T must satisfy T >= S_k + (k - 1) for all k, and the maximum over k gives the minimal feasible T. This yields the compact formula: answer = max_{k=1..N} ( S_k + k - 1 ). Each new request (a, b, p) contributes p to S_k for all k in [a, b], i.e., we perform a range add of p on the interval [a, b]. So we just need a data structure supporting range-add and global max queries on an array A[k] initially A[k] = k - 1. A segment tree with lazy propagation fits perfectly: it stores the current A[k] values, supports adding a value to a range, and can return the current maximum in O(log N). This matches the samples: after each update, the answer equals the maximum value in the segment tree. Complexity: O((N + Q) log N), which is fine for N, Q up to 1e5. \n\nPlan:\n- Build a segment tree over indices 1..N with initial values A[k] = k-1.\n- For each query (a, b, p): perform a range add of p on [a, b], then output the current maximum in the tree.\n- Use 64-bit integers to avoid overflow (p can be up to 1e9 and sums can be large).\n",
